{"session_id":"019e22eb-cdb1-7c00-9fc4-ba059c73b05e","ts":1778906879,"text":"\"For cross-machine work, the SSH tunnel is your only no-admin option:\nSSH client is already on this machine (C:\\Windows\\System32\\OpenSSH\\ssh.exe)\nOutbound connection from Windows → Mac requires no admin\nMac's Remote Login (SSH server) was already noted as enabled\nSingle command: ssh -N -L 18790:127.0.0.1:18789 logan@192.168.0.95\nUses port 18790 locally to avoid conflicting with Windows' own gateway on 18789\nWindows OpenClaw stays running independently on its own gateway\nThat tunnel gives Windows direct access to Mac's Hermes/OpenClaw session\nWant to set up that tunnel now — and wire Windows OpenClaw to be able to route tasks to Mac's Hermes via it?\nExplain in more detail what the SSH tunnel would enable us to do.\nThe user wants a detailed explanation of what the SSH tunnel would actually enable. Let me think through this carefully based on what we know about both OpenClaw and the setup.\n\nThe SSH tunnel `ssh -N -L 18790:127.0.0.1:18789 logan@192.168.0.95` would:\n- Open a local port 18790 on Windows\n- Forward that port through the SSH tunnel to Mac's 127.0.0.1:18789 (Mac's OpenClaw gateway)\n\nSo from Windows, connecting to `ws://127.0.0.1:18790` is the same as connecting to Mac's OpenClaw gateway.\n\n\nThis opens up three key possibilities: Windows Claude Code could connect its ACP bridge directly to Mac's gateway to dispatch tasks to Mac's agent with Hermes, Windows could run ACP sessions against Mac's gateway, or Windows could register as a node on Mac's gateway so Mac can dispatch shell commands back to Windows.\n\nThe real power is that Hermes on Mac can now execute tasks on Windows through the node connection when it needs Windows-specific capabilities. I can also browse Mac's OpenClaw dashboard from Windows, and if acpx is installed, fire tasks at Mac's agent sessions directly.\n\nBut I need to be clear about the limitations: the gateways don't actually federate—they're still separate systems. Hermes doesn't gain new capabilities beyond what OpenClaw's node system provides (which is essentially cmd.exe/PowerShell execution). And the tunnel requires a persistent PowerShell window unless I automate it somehow.\n\nThe most compelling use cases are where I need cross-platform task execution or want centralized control from Mac's dashboard.\n\nFor implementation, I'd need to set up the SSH tunnel on Windows, configure OpenClaw or ACP to point at the tunnel port, and ensure Mac's gateway can bind appropriately. The tunnel essentially maps Mac's port 18789 to Windows' port 18790, making Mac's gateway appear local from Windows' perspective. This creates bidirectional capability: Windows can send commands to Mac's Hermes agent, and Mac's Hermes can execute commands on Windows.\nThe tunnel does one thing: it makes Mac's OpenClaw gateway appear as a local port on Windows. ws://127.0.0.1:18790 on Windows becomes ws://127.0.0.1:18789 on Mac — the SSH connection carries the traffic invisibly.\nThat single primitive unlocks several distinct capabilities:\n\n1. Windows as a node on Mac's gateway\nopenclaw node run --host 127.0.0.1 --port 18790 --display-name \"Windows\"\nMac's Hermes agent gains the ability to run commands on Windows. From Mac's perspective, Windows becomes an execution environment it can dispatch to — run a script, read a file, call a local tool, use Windows Ollama models. Hermes on Mac can say \"run this on the Windows machine\" and it happens.\nThis is the most powerful direction: Mac's learning loop can use Windows resources.\n\n2. This Claude Code session connects to Mac's persistent agent\nRight now this Claude Code session is wired to Windows' own gateway. With the tunnel, you could instead point it at Mac's gateway:\nopenclaw acp --url ws://127.0.0.1:18790 --session agent:main:main\nThat means this coding session would share the same agent context as Mac's Hermes — same memory, same SOUL.md, same session history. Work you do in Claude Code on Windows lands in the same persistent thread that Hermes on Mac uses.\n\n3. Send tasks from Windows to Mac's Hermes\nYou could fire one-shot tasks at Mac's Hermes from the Windows command line:\nacpx openclaw 'summarize everything in ~/Documents/IDAHO-VAULT and update MEMORY.md'\nThat task runs on Mac, in Hermes, using Mac's full toolset — but you triggered it from Windows.\n\n4. Windows Ollama models accessible to Mac's Hermes\nWindows has Mistral-small, Mistral, Magistral, Devstral, Mixtral — 73GB of local models. Mac's Hermes can't directly call them today. With Windows registered as a node, Mac could invoke them via the node's shell execution: ollama run mistral \"prompt\" runs on Windows and returns output to Mac's agent.\n\nWhat it does NOT do\nIt doesn't merge the two gateways — they stay separate processes. Windows' own gateway still runs independently on port 18789\nIt doesn't give you automatic failover — if the tunnel drops, the connection breaks\nIt's not persistent across reboots without automation (you'd need to re-run the SSH command each session, or script it into startup)\nIt doesn't fix the no-Tailscale constraint — this is purely in-LAN (both machines must be on the same network, or you'd need a VPN)\n\nThe practical picture\nToday you have two isolated agents. With the tunnel + node registration:\nMac (Hermes + OpenClaw gateway)\n ↕ SSH tunnel (encrypted, port-forwarded)\nWindows (OpenClaw gateway + Ollama)\n → registered as node: Mac can run commands here\n → Claude Code sessions can attach to Mac's agent context\nOne agent that can reach both machines. Mac does the thinking (Hermes learning loop), Windows contributes execution capacity and local models.\nWorth setting up?\nOne agent, one assistant, one swarm, that can work from both machines. Yes!!\nThe user is excited and wants to set up the SSH tunnel to create a unified agent that can work from both machines. Let me plan this out step by step.\n\nThe setup needed:\n1.