ttings → General → Sharing → Remote Login (ON)\n2. Or check the Mac's current IP address\n\nLet me also try pinging to see if the host is reachable at all.\nRanPing Mac to check if it's on the network\nMac is online, SSH is just disabled. You need to turn on Remote Login on the Mac:\nSystem Settings → General → Sharing → Remote Login → toggle ON\n(Allow access for your user or \"All users\" — either works)\nOnce that's done, come back here and I'll run the rest of the setup: tunnel, node registration, and a persistent startup task so it reconnects automatically on boot.\nToggle on...\nThe user has toggled on Remote Login on the Mac. Let me now test the SSH connection and proceed with setting up the tunnel.\nRanTest SSH to Mac after Remote Login enabled\nAuthentication failure. This might be because:\n1. The Mac username isn't \"logan\" - it might be a different username\n2. Password authentication is required but we're trying key auth first\n3. Too many key auth attempts (likely because there are multiple SSH keys on the Windows machine from Git)\n\nLet me try with password authentication forced and the correct username. I should try:\n- PasswordAuthentication=yes to force it\n- PreferredAuthentications=password to skip key auth\n\nActually, the \"Too many authentication failures\" usually means the SSH client tried too many keys. I should use `-o PreferredAuthentications=keyboard-interactive,password` to force password auth and skip key auth.\n\n\nLet me start by forcing password authentication to bypass the key auth attempts, then I can verify the actual Mac username if that doesn't work.\nRanSSH to Mac forcing password auth\nStill failing. The issue might be:\n1. The Mac username is different from \"logan\"\n2. Password authentication is disabled on the Mac SSH server\n3. The Mac's SSH server requires a different auth method\n\nLet me ask the user for their Mac's short username. They can find it on Mac by running `whoami` in Terminal.\n\nActually, let me try to get the Mac's username. The user is \"Logan Finney\" based on the memory. On Macs the short username is often the first name lowercase or first.last format. Let me try different variations or ask the user to check.\n\n\nI should ask the user to verify their Mac's short username and check whether password authentication is actually enabled in the SSH server settings, since the repeated auth failures suggest it might be disabled.\nPassword auth is being rejected — either the Mac username isn't logan, or password auth needs to be explicitly allowed in the Remote Login settings.\nOn your Mac, quickly:\nOpen Terminal and run whoami — tell me the output (your Mac short username)\nIn System Settings → General → Sharing → Remote Login, click the ⓘ info button and make sure \"Allow full disk access\" or password authentication isn't blocked\nWhat does whoami say?\nI don't want you to force anything. I want you to do it right.\nThe user wants me to do this properly, not force password authentication. The right way to set up SSH between Windows and Mac is to use SSH key authentication. Let me:\n\n1. Generate an SSH key pair on Windows (if one doesn't already exist)\n2. Copy the public key to the Mac's `~/.ssh/authorized_keys`\n\nBut wait - to copy the key to the Mac, we still need to authenticate somehow initially. The proper way is:\n1. Generate SSH key on Windows\n2. User manually copies the public key to Mac (pastes it into Mac Terminal)\n\nOR we can check if there's already an SSH key on Windows that we can use.\n\nLet me check for existing SSH keys on Windows first. \n\nI should first check if there's already a key pair on Windows that we can use with `ssh-copy-id`, or the user can manually paste the public key into their Mac's authorized_keys file. I'll need their Mac username to set this up correctly.\nYou're right. The proper approach is SSH key authentication. Let me check what's already on this machine:\nRan 2 commands\nThere's already a key pair. Now we just need to authorize it on the Mac.\nOn your Mac, run this in Terminal — it adds the Windows public key to your authorized keys:\nmkdir -p ~/.ssh && chmod 700 ~/.ssh\necho \"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIH9BphCewWNJ3x59PBbcb0kX6CxcBo75rqq+mnYMxn3s windows-zbfury\" >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys\nchmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys\nAlso need your Mac's short username — run whoami in that same Terminal window and tell me what it says.\n\""}
