{"session_id":"019e22eb-cdb1-7c00-9fc4-ba059c73b05e","ts":1778909331,"text":"WINDOWS CODEX SAYS: **Quick Research Note**\nWhile we wait for Mac Codex, the useful finding is: our tunnel/token problem is exactly aligned with current OpenClaw docs. For remote node hosts over SSH tunnel, the node side needs `OPENCLAW_GATEWAY_TOKEN` set from the gateway host’s `gateway.auth.token`, then runs `openclaw node run --host 127.0.0.1 --port 18790 ...`. OpenClaw docs also clarify that device pairing is separate from the older `openclaw nodes pending/approve` store, so Mac may need `openclaw devices list` / `openclaw devices approve <requestId>` rather than only `openclaw nodes pending`.\n\n**Landscape Read**\nOpenClaw is best understood as a local-first personal agent gateway: channels in, model/tool runtime in the middle, node hosts as remote execution surfaces. Its strength is practical “assistant everywhere” operation; its weakness is that the security boundary is the whole local operator trust domain. The vault’s own notes already point the right direction: loopback bind, SSH tunnel, token auth, strict config validation, local mesh/Ollama fallback, and strong suspicion of unvetted skills.\n\nHermes Agent looks like the most direct OpenClaw peer/possible successor path. Current GitHub README says Hermes has native Windows PowerShell support in early beta, seven terminal backends, messaging gateway support, persistent memory, skills, MCP, cron, and an explicit `hermes claw migrate` path from OpenClaw. That is important because older local harvested docs say Windows was not supported and required WSL2; the current upstream appears to have moved past that.\n\nFor the broader agentic swarm/personal assistant field in May 2026, the convergence is pretty clear:\n\n- OpenClaw/Hermes: personal assistant gateway, messaging surfaces, skills, local execution, persistent memory.\n- CrewAI: production workflows via Flows plus agent Crews, good for controlled multi-agent business automation.\n- Microsoft AutoGen / Agent Framework: event-driven multi-agent apps, observability, type safety, graph workflows.\n- OpenAI Agents SDK: model-native agent harness, sandbox execution, MCP/skills/AGENTS.md primitives, snapshot/rehydration.\n- IDAHO-VAULT: already leans toward a stronger answer than “more agents”: durable vault files, SQLite/blackboard field, deterministic guard layer, authority boundaries, and human approval gates.\n\n**Local Fit**\nThe vault’s existing architecture is healthy here. [BIG IFS — UNIFIED SWARM.md](C:/Users/loganf/Documents/IDAHO-VAULT/BIG%20IFS%20%E2%80%94%20UNIFIED%20SWARM.md) says the real missing layer is a common interface, shared memory bus, handoff protocol, observability, and human-on-the-loop checkpoints. [!-REPORT-STIGMERGIC-IMPLEMENTATION-PATHS-2026-04-22.md](C:/Users/loganf/Documents/IDAHO-VAULT/!-REPORT-STIGMERGIC-IMPLEMENTATION-PATHS-2026-04-22.md) sharpens that: don’t build a giant swarm OS; build a smaller durable coordination medium with guardrails and decay.\n\n**Security Posture**\nThe live rule should stay conservative: no public gateway exposure, no unreviewed community skills, no shared hostile-user gateway, no silent broad `system.run`. VirusTotal’s February 2026 analysis frames OpenClaw skills as a real supply-chain malware channel, and OpenClaw’s own security docs explicitly say it is a personal assistant trust model, not a hostile multi-tenant boundary.\n\nSources: [OpenClaw Nodes](https://openclaw.cc/en/nodes/), [OpenClaw Security](https://docs.openclaw.ai/gateway/security), [Hermes Agent GitHub](https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent), [CrewAI docs](https://docs.crewai.com/en/introduction), [Microsoft AutoGen](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/project/autogen/), [OpenAI Agents SDK update](https://openai.com/index/the-next-evolution-of-the-agents-sdk/), [VirusTotal OpenClaw skills analysis](https://blog.virustotal.com/2026/02/from-automation-to-infection-how.html)."}
